## Findings

### ⚠️ Warns
1. ⚠️ Gate 03 TS-11 (with AL-17): with the new shared child budget, a slow submission's score depends on which checks reach the lock first, not on the submission. tests/test_output.py:8084 `_CHILD_SECONDS = {"left": 3000.0}`, :8101-8103 `allowed = min(timeout, _CHILD_SECONDS["left"])` / `if allowed <= 0: raise subprocess.TimeoutExpired(...)`; rewardkit 0.2.1 runs up to 8 programmatic criteria at once (runner.py:658 `max_concurrent_programmatic: int = 8`), so after 3,000 s thread order at the flock (:8099-8100) decides which checks lose. This only hits submissions whose children together run past 3,000 s (the oracle's whole verifier took ~119 s). Suggestion/Improvement. Fix: in tests/test_output.py replace the first-come pool at :8084/:8101 with fixed per-child caps that add up to less than 3,000 s (scale the `timeout` arguments at :8204, :9809, :9973), so a slow submission loses the same checks on every run.
2. ⚠️ Gate 02 DS-14 note / Gate 05 AL-18 (one item): the oracle's fix note no longer describes the planner it installs. solution/solve.py:723 FIX_NOTES "core.planner: search over (schema, field states) with a heap ordered by (cost, edge-id list); ..." vs the new planner at solve.py:102-318 (backward bit sets, forward rebuild). The note is written into /app/out/module_core.json `notes` and repair-report.json `fixes` (solve.py:1183, :1189). Nothing grades it (K19 `notes` is free-form). Suggestion/Improvement. Fix: reword solve.py:723 to "core.planner: exact least remaining cost per (schema, field states) computed backwards over bit sets, plan rebuilt forwards by smallest edge id (M6 to M8, K14)".

